(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[7] = {"grade":7,"packs":{"grade7-english":{"g7eng-listening":[{"id":"g7eng-listening-003","q":"The teacher says: \"We will now proceed to the hall.\" From the context, what does <b>proceed</b> mean?","opts":["run","go","return","wait"],"a":"go","hint":"The sentence describes moving from one place to another.","exp":"\"<b>Proceed</b>\" means to move forward or go. Context clues — the hall as a destination — help you work out the meaning without a dictionary. Learning vocabulary from context is an important listening skill."},{"id":"g7eng-listening-010","q":"You hear: \"The councillor was <b>adamant</b> that the new road would not be built through the park.\" What does <b>adamant</b> mean?","opts":["quietly hopeful about the outcome","willing to listen and to compromise","completely firm and refusing to change","uncertain and confused about it all"],"a":"completely firm and refusing to change","hint":"The councillor is making a strong, final-sounding statement.","exp":"\"<b>Adamant</b>\" means absolutely firm or refusing to change one's position. It is stronger than \"determined\" — it implies no possibility of changing one's mind. The context (refusing the road) supports a meaning of total firmness."},{"id":"g7eng-listening-019","q":"A speaker says: <i>\"We had booked the coach and printed the tickets, but the trip fell through at the last minute.\"</i><br>What does <b>fell through</b> mean here?","opts":["started early","went very well","did not happen","cost too much"],"a":"did not happen","hint":"The coach was booked and the tickets were printed — and then?","exp":"\"To fall through\" means to fail to happen after being arranged. The word \"but\" warns you that the news turns bad, so \"went very well\" is the opposite, and the sentence mentions neither the time nor the price."},{"id":"g7eng-hd-003","q":"A speaker says: <i>\"The plan is sound, but the timing is unfortunate.\"</i><br>What does \"sound\" mean here?","opts":["Noisy in a pleasant way","Sensible and well judged","Fast and efficient","Loud and easily heard"],"a":"Sensible and well judged","hint":"The word has several meanings. Which fits a plan?","exp":"Applied to a plan, <b>sound</b> means solid and well judged. The everyday meaning of noise does not fit the sentence — context, not frequency, decides which sense is meant."},{"id":"g7eng-hd-001","q":"A speaker says: <i>\"The bus is usually on time, though I would leave early today if I were you.\"</i><br>What is the speaker really telling you?","opts":["The bus has already left","The bus is always reliable","You should catch a later bus","Something may be different about today"],"a":"Something may be different about today","hint":"The word \"though\" turns the sentence. What does it turn toward?","exp":"\"Usually\" and \"though\" together signal an exception. The speaker is warning that <b>today may not follow the usual pattern</b> — the advice is the message, not the reassurance in front of it."}],"g7eng-speaking":[{"id":"g7eng-speaking-017","q":"In a discussion you agree with Kavi and you also want to add a reason of your own. Which reply does BOTH?","opts":["\"That is one way of seeing it.\"","\"I would rather not comment.\"","\"I agree with Kavi completely.\"","\"I agree, and I would add that…\""],"a":"\"I agree, and I would add that…\"","hint":"Look for the reply that signals agreement and then opens a new point.","exp":"Only the first reply agrees and then extends the idea, which is how a discussion moves forward. The second agrees but adds nothing, the third is neutral and hides your own view, and the fourth withdraws from the discussion altogether."},{"id":"g7eng-speaking-020","q":"You need a classmate's ruler during a revision session. Which request is the MOST polite?","opts":["\"Pass the ruler over here.\"","\"I need your ruler now.\"","\"Give me your ruler, please.\"","\"Could I borrow your ruler?\""],"a":"\"Could I borrow your ruler?\"","hint":"Politeness comes from asking, not from the word \"please\" on its own.","exp":"\"Could I…?\" asks permission and leaves the other person free to say no. \"Give me… please\" is still a command with \"please\" attached to it, and the last two are direct orders with no request in them at all."},{"id":"g7eng-speaking-018","q":"You want to make clear that it was <b>Sara</b>, not Reeya, who borrowed your calculator. Which word should you stress in \"Sara borrowed my calculator\"?","opts":["borrowed","Sara","calculator","my"],"a":"Sara","hint":"The stress falls on the word that is being corrected.","exp":"Stressing SARA answers the question \"who?\" and corrects the wrong name. Stressing \"borrowed\" would correct the action (borrowed, not stole), \"my\" would correct the owner, and \"calculator\" would correct which object was taken."},{"id":"g7eng-hd-010","q":"The sentence <i>\"You finished the whole thing\"</i> is said with a rising tone at the end.<br>What does that change?","opts":["It becomes a question expressing surprise","It changes nothing about the meaning","It becomes a firmer statement of fact","It becomes an order to finish something"],"a":"It becomes a question expressing surprise","hint":"Rising intonation does a job that punctuation does in writing.","exp":"A rising tone turns a statement into a <b>question</b>, often carrying surprise. Speech uses intonation where writing uses a question mark — the same words, two meanings."},{"id":"g7eng-hd-012","q":"Which sentence would sound out of place in a formal school assembly speech?","opts":["We are grateful for your continued support.","It is my pleasure to welcome you today.","We had a really awesome time, guys.","Allow me to thank the organising committee."],"a":"We had a really awesome time, guys.","hint":"Look for the vocabulary and address that belong to conversation.","exp":"\"Awesome\" and \"guys\" belong to casual speech among friends. The others keep the <b>formal register</b> an assembly expects. Register is about matching the setting, not about long words."}],"g7eng-reading":[{"id":"g7eng-reading-003","q":"Read: \"The hiker was <b>exhausted</b> after the long climb. She sank into her sleeping bag without even eating.\" From the context, what does <b>exhausted</b> mean?","opts":["very hungry","very happy","extremely tired","slightly cold"],"a":"extremely tired","hint":"\"Sank into her sleeping bag without eating\" — what would make someone do this?","exp":"\"<b>Exhausted</b>\" means extremely tired. Context clues: the hiker climbed a long way and went straight to sleep without eating — both suggest extreme tiredness, not hunger or happiness."},{"id":"g7eng-reading-004","q":"Read: \"The shop was <b>thriving</b> — every day was busy, customers queued outside, and the owner had to hire two more staff.\" From the context, what does <b>thriving</b> mean?","opts":["barely surviving","closing down","just opening","doing very well"],"a":"doing very well","hint":"What do busy queues and extra staff tell you about how well the shop is doing?","exp":"\"<b>Thriving</b>\" means doing very well or being very successful. Context clues: busy days, queues of customers, need for more staff — all point to great success."},{"id":"g7eng-reading-020","q":"Read: <i>\"Preheat the oven. Sift the flour into a bowl. Add the sugar slowly, then beat for two minutes.\"</i><br>What TYPE of text is this?","opts":["A newspaper report","A personal diary","A set of instructions","A short story"],"a":"A set of instructions","hint":"Look at the verbs and ask who they are addressed to.","exp":"Every sentence begins with a command verb and the steps come in order, which is the shape of instructions or a recipe. A report would tell you what happened, a diary would use \"I\", and a story would give you characters and events."},{"id":"g7eng-reading-006","q":"Read: \"Despite the rain and muddy paths, the children were <b>undaunted</b> — they kept walking, laughing all the way to the top.\" From the context, what does <b>undaunted</b> mean?","opts":["frightened and cautious","not discouraged; carrying on bravely","very wet and tired","uncertain which path to take"],"a":"not discouraged; carrying on bravely","hint":"\"Despite the difficulties, they kept going\" — what word describes continuing confidently despite problems?","exp":"\"<b>Undaunted</b>\" means not frightened or discouraged — continuing bravely in spite of difficulties. \"Despite\" introduces the obstacles, but the children laughed and carried on, which shows they were not put off."},{"id":"g7eng-hd-017","q":"<i>\"Ravi checked the sky twice before leaving, took the umbrella from behind the door, and told his mother he might be late.\"</i><br>What can you infer?","opts":["He was angry with his mother","He had forgotten something at home","He was going out to meet friends","He expected rain and a longer journey"],"a":"He expected rain and a longer journey","hint":"Three actions. What single expectation explains all three?","exp":"Checking the sky and taking an umbrella point to rain; warning about lateness follows from it. <b>Inference means the explanation that fits every detail</b>, not just one."}]},"grade7-french":{"g7fr-co":[{"id":"g7fr-co-012","q":"L'intonation sert à :","opts":["Écrire sans fautes","Trouver les synonymes","Exprimer des émotions","Conjuguer les verbes"],"a":"Exprimer des émotions","hint":"L'intonation, c'est la façon dont la voix monte ou descend.","exp":"L'<b>intonation</b> est la variation de la hauteur de la voix. Elle permet d'exprimer la joie, la surprise, la colère ou de distinguer une question d'une affirmation."},{"id":"g7fr-co-016","q":"Quel type de texte oral utilise principalement des verbes à l'impératif ?","opts":["Le texte descriptif","Le texte informatif","Le texte narratif","Le texte injonctif"],"a":"Le texte injonctif","hint":"L'impératif exprime un ordre ou une instruction.","exp":"Le texte <b>injonctif</b> donne des ordres ou des instructions et utilise fréquemment l'impératif (ex. : \"Mélangez\", \"Tournez à droite\", \"Ne touchez pas\") ou l'infinitif."},{"id":"g7fr-co-019","q":"Dans une interview, le journaliste est principalement :","opts":["Uniquement l'émetteur","Toujours le récepteur","Ni locuteur ni interlocuteur","Locuteur puis interlocuteur"],"a":"Locuteur puis interlocuteur","hint":"Dans une interview, les rôles s'inversent constamment.","exp":"Dans une interview, le journaliste et l'invité échangent les rôles : le journaliste est d'abord <b>locuteur</b> (pose la question) puis <b>interlocuteur</b> (écoute la réponse), et vice versa."},{"id":"g7fr-hd-007","q":"Un fr&egrave;re dit &agrave; sa s&oelig;ur : <i>&laquo; Tu as encore laiss&eacute; la fen&ecirc;tre ouverte pendant l'averse... Bravo ! &raquo;</i> Quelle est son intention ?","opts":["il exprime un reproche sur un ton ironique","il félicite sincèrement sa sœur","il demande poliment que la fenêtre soit fermée","il pose une question à sa sœur"],"a":"il exprime un reproche sur un ton ironique","hint":"Le mot &laquo; Bravo &raquo; ne veut pas toujours dire ce qu'il semble dire.","exp":"Les points de suspension et le contexte (une fen&ecirc;tre ouverte sous la pluie) montrent que &laquo; Bravo &raquo; est dit &agrave; l'envers : c'est de l'<b>ironie</b>, donc un reproche d&eacute;guis&eacute;."},{"id":"g7fr-hd-002","q":"La radio diffuse : <i>&laquo; Un avis de fortes pluies est en vigueur. La population des r&eacute;gions basses est invit&eacute;e &agrave; ne pas traverser les rivi&egrave;res. &raquo;</i> Ce texte oral est :","opts":["un communiqué officiel de sécurité","une critique de film à la radio","un extrait d'un conte traditionnel","une conversation téléphonique privée"],"a":"un communiqué officiel de sécurité","hint":"Le message vient d'une autorit&eacute; et s'adresse &agrave; toute la population, pas &agrave; une personne pr&eacute;cise.","exp":"Un avis impersonnel qui informe et recommande une conduite &agrave; toute la population est un <b>communiqu&eacute;</b> officiel. Un &eacute;change priv&eacute; ou un conte n'emploieraient jamais &laquo; est en vigueur &raquo;."}],"g7fr-eo":[{"id":"g7fr-eo-006","q":"Quand on parle de soi à l'oral, quel pronom doit-on utiliser ?","opts":["Tu","Nous","Il","Je"],"a":"Je","hint":"Pour parler de soi-même, on utilise la première personne du singulier.","exp":"La première personne du singulier <b>\"je\"</b> est utilisée lorsqu'on parle de soi-même, de ses expériences et de ses opinions."},{"id":"g7fr-eo-008","q":"Lorsqu'on hésite en parlant, quelle expression peut-on utiliser pour gagner du temps ?","opts":["\"Vite, il faut finir !\"","\"Tant pis, j'abandonne.\"","\"C'est-à-dire...\"","\"Euh... euh... euh...\""],"a":"\"C'est-à-dire...\"","hint":"Ces expressions permettent de reformuler et de mieux expliquer.","exp":"Des expressions comme <b>\"c'est-à-dire\"</b> ou <b>\"en d'autres termes\"</b> permettent de reformuler une idée, de gagner du temps et de clarifier sa pensée."},{"id":"g7fr-eo-018","q":"Si on perd le fil de ses idées en parlant, quelle est la meilleure réaction ?","opts":["Répéter la même phrase en boucle","Quitter la salle immédiatement","Se taire et regarder par terre","Faire une pause et continuer"],"a":"Faire une pause et continuer","hint":"Une pause bien gérée montre du sang-froid, pas une faiblesse.","exp":"Perdre le fil est normal. La meilleure réaction est de <b>faire une pause</b>, respirer, reformuler et continuer. Les auditeurs apprécient le calme plus que la précipitation."},{"id":"g7fr-hd-012","q":"Vous lisez &agrave; voix haute : <i>&laquo; Quand le vent est tomb&eacute;, les p&ecirc;cheurs de Mah&eacute;bourg ont repris la mer. &raquo;</i> O&ugrave; faut-il marquer la pause principale ?","opts":["après « pêcheurs », entre le sujet et son complément","après « tombé », à la fin de la subordonnée","après « vent », juste avant le verbe « est tombé »","après « repris », juste avant « la mer »"],"a":"après « tombé », à la fin de la subordonnée","hint":"La virgule &eacute;crite correspond ici &agrave; la respiration du lecteur.","exp":"La proposition subordonn&eacute;e de temps se termine &agrave; &laquo; tomb&eacute; &raquo;, o&ugrave; la virgule marque la fronti&egrave;re de sens. Couper entre un sujet et son verbe rendrait la phrase incompr&eacute;hensible &agrave; l'oreille."},{"id":"g7fr-hd-013","q":"Lue en insistant fortement sur le mot &laquo; toi &raquo;, la phrase <i>&laquo; C'est toi qui as rang&eacute; la salle ? &raquo;</i> exprime surtout :","opts":["une simple demande d'information neutre","un ordre donné à celui qui écoute","un reproche adressé à toute la classe présente","la surprise devant l'identité de la personne"],"a":"la surprise devant l'identité de la personne","hint":"L'accent d'insistance porte sur celui qui a fait l'action.","exp":"Appuyer sur &laquo; toi &raquo; oppose cette personne &agrave; toutes les autres : le lecteur exprime son <b>&eacute;tonnement</b> que ce soit elle. La m&ecirc;me phrase dite platement serait une simple question."}],"g7fr-ce":[{"id":"g7fr-ce-003","q":"Les idées secondaires dans un texte servent à :","opts":["Compléter l'idée principale","Contredire l'auteur du texte","Former le titre du texte","Remplacer l'idée principale"],"a":"Compléter l'idée principale","hint":"Les idées secondaires apportent des détails supplémentaires.","exp":"Les <b>idées secondaires</b> développent l'idée principale en ajoutant des exemples, des précisions ou des explications."},{"id":"g7fr-ce-006","q":"Dans la phrase : \"Marie est née le 5 mars 2010 à Port-Louis\", quelle information est explicite ?","opts":["La date de naissance de Marie","La couleur préférée de Marie","Les études de Marie","Le métier de Marie"],"a":"La date de naissance de Marie","hint":"La date est clairement mentionnée dans la phrase.","exp":"La phrase dit clairement que Marie est née <b>le 5 mars 2010</b>. Cette information est directement présente — c'est une information explicite."},{"id":"g7fr-ce-002","q":"Comment trouve-t-on l'idée principale d'un texte ?","opts":["En lisant la première phrase","En lisant le texte en entier","En regardant les images","En comptant les mots du texte"],"a":"En lisant le texte en entier","hint":"La question \"De quoi parle ce texte ?\" guide vers l'idée principale.","exp":"Pour dégager l'<b>idée principale</b>, on lit le texte en entier, puis on se demande : \"Quel est le sujet central ?\" ou \"Qu'est-ce que l'auteur veut dire essentiellement ?\""},{"id":"g7fr-ce-008","q":"Lisez : \"Le colibri, le plus petit oiseau du monde, bat ses ailes jusqu'à 80 fois par seconde.\" Quelle information est explicite ?","opts":["Le colibri vit à l'île Maurice","Le colibri mange surtout des fleurs","Le colibri est le plus petit oiseau","Le colibri est un oiseau en danger"],"a":"Le colibri est le plus petit oiseau","hint":"Lisez la phrase attentivement — quelle donnée chiffrée est mentionnée ?","exp":"La phrase énonce explicitement deux choses : que le colibri est <b>le plus petit oiseau du monde</b>, et qu'il bat des ailes jusqu'à 80 fois par seconde. Les autres options ne sont mentionnées nulle part dans la phrase."},{"id":"g7fr-ce-018","q":"Pour répondre à une question de compréhension écrite, la meilleure méthode est :","opts":["Inventer une réponse de tête","Copier un paragraphe entier","Répondre sans relire le texte","Repérer les mots-clés du texte"],"a":"Repérer les mots-clés du texte","hint":"Les mots-clés de la question guident vers l'endroit où se trouve la réponse.","exp":"La méthode efficace : identifier les <b>mots-clés</b> de la question, les localiser dans le texte, lire ce passage attentivement, puis reformuler la réponse avec ses propres mots."}]},"grade7-maths":{"g7m-integers":[{"id":"g7m-integers-003","q":"Which integer is 5 steps to the LEFT of −2 on a number line?","opts":["−7","7","3","−3"],"a":"−7","hint":"Moving left on a number line means subtracting.","exp":"−2 − 5 = −7. Moving left means decreasing, so the answer is −7."},{"id":"g7m-integers-017","q":"Rishi says that −7 is greater than −3 \"because 7 is bigger than 3\". Which statement puts him right?","opts":["−3 is less than −7","−7 is equal to −3","−7 is less than −3","−7 is greater than −3"],"a":"−7 is less than −3","hint":"On a number line the further LEFT a number sits, the smaller it is.","exp":"−7 lies further left than −3, so −7 < −3. The size of the digit tells you nothing on its own once a minus sign is there: the last two options both repeat Rishi's mistake, and the middle one claims the two numbers are the same point."},{"id":"g7m-integers-013","q":"What is (−5) + (−8) − (−3)?","opts":["−16","−6","0","−10"],"a":"−10","hint":"Subtracting a negative is the same as adding a positive.","exp":"(−5) + (−8) = −13. Then −13 − (−3) = −13 + 3 = −10."},{"id":"g7m-integers-014","q":"At noon the temperature was 7°C. By midnight it had fallen by 11°C. What was the midnight temperature?","opts":["4°C","18°C","−11°C","−4°C"],"a":"−4°C","hint":"7 − 11 = ?","exp":"7 − 11 = −4°C. The temperature dropped below zero."},{"id":"g7m-hd-003","q":"Rajesh has <b>Rs 450</b> in his account. He pays a bill of <b>Rs 780</b>, then deposits <b>Rs 200</b>.<br>What is his balance?","opts":["−Rs 530","−Rs 130","Rs 130","Rs 1,430"],"a":"−Rs 130","hint":"A balance can go below zero. Follow the two transactions in order.","exp":"450 − 780 = −330, then −330 + 200 = <b>−Rs 130</b>. He is overdrawn. Reading the answer as +130 is the error of ignoring the sign."}],"g7m-operations":[{"id":"g7m-operations-006","q":"What is the value of 36 ÷ 4 + 2 × 3?","opts":["12","33","21","15"],"a":"15","hint":"Division and multiplication before addition.","exp":"36 ÷ 4 = 9; 2 × 3 = 6; then 9 + 6 = 15."},{"id":"g7m-operations-020","q":"Which of these statements is NOT always true?","opts":["20 ÷ 4 = 4 ÷ 20","20 + 0 = 0 + 20","20 + 4 = 4 + 20","20 × 4 = 4 × 20"],"a":"20 ÷ 4 = 4 ÷ 20","hint":"Addition and multiplication can be reordered freely. Can division?","exp":"20 ÷ 4 = 5 but 4 ÷ 20 = 0.2, so division is not commutative. Addition and multiplication both are, which makes the other three true for any numbers you choose."},{"id":"g7m-operations-013","q":"Nita worked out 18 − 6 ÷ 3 and wrote 4. What did she do wrong?","opts":["She divided before subtracting","She subtracted before dividing","She added instead of subtracting","She multiplied before dividing"],"a":"She subtracted before dividing","hint":"Undo her answer: which pair of numbers must she have combined first to reach 4?","exp":"She calculated (18 − 6) ÷ 3 = 4. Division outranks subtraction, so the correct route is 6 ÷ 3 = 2 first, then 18 − 2 = 16. Dividing first is exactly what she should have done, and there is no addition or multiplication anywhere in the expression."},{"id":"g7m-hd-012","q":"A box holds <b>24</b> pencils. A school buys <b>15</b> boxes and gives <b>90</b> pencils to another school.<br>How many are left?","opts":["264","450","126","270"],"a":"270","hint":"Find the total bought first, then take away.","exp":"15 × 24 = 360, then 360 − 90 = <b>270</b>. Subtracting before multiplying, 15 × (24 − 90), is meaningless here and is what the brackets rule prevents."},{"id":"g7m-hd-015","q":"Evaluate: <b>(48 ÷ 6) + 5 × (9 − 6)</b>","opts":["39","23","21","48"],"a":"23","hint":"Two brackets, then multiplication, then addition.","exp":"48 ÷ 6 = 8 and (9 − 6) = 3, so 8 + 5 × 3 = 8 + 15 = <b>23</b>. Adding 8 + 5 first and then multiplying by 3 gives 39."}],"g7m-indices":[{"id":"g7m-indices-008","q":"Which is the prime factorisation of 48?","opts":["4 × 12","2³ × 3²","2³ × 6","2⁴ × 3"],"a":"2⁴ × 3","hint":"48 = 2 × 24 = 2 × 2 × 12 = …","exp":"48 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 = 2⁴ × 3. Only prime bases are used."},{"id":"g7m-indices-015","q":"Bimal wrote 4³ = 12 in his exercise book. What mistake did he make?","opts":["He added 4 and 3","He squared 4 not cubed","He divided 4 into 3","He multiplied 4 by 3"],"a":"He multiplied 4 by 3","hint":"Work out what 4³ really means, then ask what calculation gives 12.","exp":"4 × 3 = 12, so he treated the index as an ordinary factor; 4³ actually means 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. Adding gives 7, dividing gives a fraction, and squaring would have given 16 — none of them 12."},{"id":"g7m-indices-011","q":"Which is greater: 2⁵ or 4³?","opts":["2⁵","4³","They are equal","Cannot compare"],"a":"4³","hint":"Calculate each value: 2⁵ = ? and 4³ = ?","exp":"2⁵ = 32. 4³ = 64. Since 64 > 32, 4³ is greater."},{"id":"g7m-hd-017","q":"A cube has edges of <b>4 cm</b>. It is cut into small cubes of edge <b>1 cm</b>.<br>How many small cubes are there?","opts":["64","16","48","12"],"a":"64","hint":"A cube has three dimensions, not two.","exp":"4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = <b>64</b>. Using 4² = 16 counts only one layer; there are four such layers."},{"id":"g7m-hd-020","q":"Evaluate: <b>3² + 4² − 2³</b>","opts":["17","25","19","13"],"a":"17","hint":"Work out each power first, then add and subtract.","exp":"9 + 16 − 8 = <b>17</b>. Reading 2³ as 6 instead of 8 gives 19 — multiplying the base by the index rather than repeating it."}]},"grade7-science":{"g7s-inquiry":[{"id":"g7s-inquiry-010","q":"In a scientific investigation, what is a hypothesis?","opts":["The final conclusion","A testable prediction","A graph of the results","The list of equipment"],"a":"A testable prediction","hint":"It usually takes the form \"If … then … because …\".","exp":"A hypothesis is a <b>testable prediction</b> made before the experiment. It says what you expect to happen and why, and it must be possible to support or disprove it with evidence. The conclusion comes afterwards."},{"id":"g7s-inquiry-012","q":"A student investigates how the height of a ramp affects the speed of a toy car. She keeps the same car, the same surface and the same release point each time. Why?","opts":["To finish the test faster","To keep the test fair","Because she has one car","Because she was told to"],"a":"To keep the test fair","hint":"Changing only one variable at a time isolates its effect.","exp":"Keeping everything else constant makes it a <b>fair test</b>: any difference in the car's speed must then be due to the ramp height alone and not to some other factor."},{"id":"g7s-inquiry-016","q":"A student's experiment gives results that do not support her hypothesis. What should she do?","opts":["Repeat until they fit","Change the results to fit","Reconsider the hypothesis","Ignore the odd results"],"a":"Reconsider the hypothesis","hint":"In science, disproving a hypothesis is just as valuable as proving one.","exp":"Good science requires honesty. If the results do not support the hypothesis, the scientist should analyse the data and <b>reconsider the hypothesis</b>. Changing, ignoring or repeating until you get the answer you wanted is falsifying data, and is never acceptable."},{"id":"g7s-hd-001","q":"A pupil tests whether fertiliser helps plants grow. She gives one plant fertiliser and puts it on a sunny windowsill, and leaves the other plain on a shelf in the corner.<br>Why can she not trust her result?","opts":["Fertiliser always works, so the test is pointless","Plants cannot be compared with one another fairly","Two things differ, so neither can be blamed","She should have used three plants instead of two"],"a":"Two things differ, so neither can be blamed","hint":"Count how many things are different between the two plants.","exp":"Fertiliser <b>and</b> light both differ, so a difference in growth could be caused by either. A fair test changes one variable and holds everything else constant."},{"id":"g7s-hd-008","q":"Why are safety goggles worn even for an experiment that involves no obvious chemicals?","opts":["Goggles keep dust off the apparatus being used","Glassware and hot liquids can fail without warning","School rules require them for every single lesson","Goggles help pupils see fine detail more clearly"],"a":"Glassware and hot liquids can fail without warning","hint":"The risk is not only what is in the beaker.","exp":"Glass cracks, liquids spit and springs slip. Eye protection guards against the <b>unexpected</b>, which by definition you cannot see coming — the reason it is worn before anything goes wrong."}],"g7s-measurement":[{"id":"g7s-measurement-011","q":"Which instrument is used to measure mass accurately in a laboratory?","opts":["Spring balance","Electronic balance","Measuring cylinder","Stopwatch timer"],"a":"Electronic balance","hint":"This instrument measures the amount of matter in an object.","exp":"An <b>electronic balance</b> measures mass accurately. A spring balance measures force (weight) rather than mass and is far less precise; a measuring cylinder measures volume."},{"id":"g7s-measurement-013","q":"A swimming pool is 25 m long, 10 m wide and 2 m deep. What is its volume?","opts":["500 m³","37 m³","5000 m³","250 m³"],"a":"500 m³","hint":"Volume = length × width × depth.","exp":"Volume = 25 × 10 × 2 = <b>500 m³</b>. The formula for the volume of a cuboid (rectangular prism) is length × width × height."},{"id":"g7s-hd-022","q":"Why do scientists worldwide agree to use SI units?","opts":["SI units give smaller numbers than other systems","Each country would otherwise measure nothing","SI units are easier to write down by hand","Results can be compared and repeated anywhere"],"a":"Results can be compared and repeated anywhere","hint":"Science depends on other people checking your work.","exp":"A shared system means a result from Mauritius can be <b>checked in Canada</b> without conversion errors. Agreement on units is what makes results transferable."},{"id":"g7s-hd-024","q":"A pupil measures the thickness of one sheet of paper by measuring 100 sheets and dividing by 100.<br>Why is this better than measuring one sheet?","opts":["Paper thickness changes from sheet to sheet","A ruler cannot measure anything below 1 cm","One hundred sheets are easier to hold still","The measuring error is shared across all 100 sheets"],"a":"The measuring error is shared across all 100 sheets","hint":"The ruler error is the same size whether you measure one sheet or a hundred.","exp":"A ±0.5 mm reading error on one sheet is enormous; spread over 100 sheets it becomes ±0.005 mm each. Measuring <b>many and dividing</b> is standard for very small quantities."},{"id":"g7s-hd-014","q":"A block measures 4 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm and has a mass of 48 g.<br>What is its density?","opts":["1152 g/cm³","48 g/cm³","2 g/cm³","24 g/cm³"],"a":"2 g/cm³","hint":"Find the volume first, then divide the mass by it.","exp":"Volume = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cm³. Density = 48 ÷ 24 = <b>2 g/cm³</b>. The commonest error is dividing by one side instead of the volume."}],"g7s-cells":[{"id":"g7s-cells-010","q":"Which organelle is responsible for protein synthesis (making proteins)?","opts":["Nucleus","Mitochondrion","Vacuole","Ribosome"],"a":"Ribosome","hint":"Proteins are assembled here using instructions from the DNA.","exp":"<b>Ribosomes</b> are small organelles found in the cytoplasm where proteins are made. They read the instructions (mRNA) from the nucleus and assemble amino acids into protein chains."},{"id":"g7s-cells-020","q":"Onion cells keep a fixed, brick-like shape but cheek cells do not. Which part explains the difference?","opts":["Cytoplasm","Cell membrane","Nucleus","Cell wall"],"a":"Cell wall","hint":"Which structure does an onion cell have that a cheek cell has not?","exp":"Only the plant cell has a rigid cell wall of cellulose outside the membrane, and that is what holds the brick shape. Both cells have a membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus, so none of those can be the difference."},{"id":"g7s-hd-033","q":"Both plant and animal cells have a cell membrane, cytoplasm and a nucleus.<br>What does sharing these suggest?","opts":["Plants and animals are really the same thing","These parts do nothing important in either","Animal cells developed from plant cells","All these parts are essential to staying alive"],"a":"All these parts are essential to staying alive","hint":"Why would two very different organisms keep the same structures?","exp":"Features found in <b>every</b> cell are the ones life cannot do without — control, chemistry and a boundary. The differences show what each kind of life does in addition."},{"id":"g7s-hd-034","q":"A plant that has not been watered wilts, then recovers fully after watering.<br>What happened inside its cells?","opts":["The cell walls dissolved and then reformed","Vacuoles lost water, then refilled and became firm","Chloroplasts stopped working and then restarted","The cells died and were replaced by new ones"],"a":"Vacuoles lost water, then refilled and became firm","hint":"Something inside the cell provides the firmness that holds a stem up.","exp":"A full <b>vacuole</b> presses the cell contents against the wall and keeps the plant rigid. Lose that water and the plant droops; restore it and the plant stands up again."},{"id":"g7s-hd-032","q":"Nerve cells are very long, sometimes almost a metre.<br>Why is that shape useful?","opts":["Long cells are much stronger than short ones","It allows the cell to store more information","A signal can travel far without being passed on","Length keeps the cell warmer than its surroundings"],"a":"A signal can travel far without being passed on","hint":"Every handover between cells costs time.","exp":"One long cell carries a signal a long way <b>without a handover</b>, and each handover would slow the message. Shape again follows function."}]},"grade7-social-modern-studies":{"g7sms-islands":[{"id":"g7sms-islands-003","q":"Rodrigues island is located approximately how many kilometres east of Mauritius?","opts":["100 km","1,000 km","50 km","560 km"],"a":"560 km","hint":"Rodrigues is the most distant island of the republic from the main island.","exp":"Rodrigues is located approximately <b>560 km east</b> of Mauritius island. It is an autonomous region within the Republic of Mauritius."},{"id":"g7sms-islands-013","q":"Saint Brandon (Cargados Carajos Shoals) matters to Mauritius mainly for one reason. Which?","opts":["It extends the maritime zone","It holds the largest population","It is the closest to Africa","It has the highest mountains"],"a":"It extends the maritime zone","hint":"Remote island territories often have strategic value for ocean resources.","exp":"<b>Saint Brandon</b> is a group of low-lying coral islets about 400 km north of Mauritius. While largely uninhabited, it is important for <b>extending Mauritius's maritime zone</b> and for fishing operations in the surrounding waters."},{"id":"g7sms-islands-011","q":"The Black River Gorges in Mauritius is an example of which landform?","opts":["A sand delta","A coral reef","A deep valley","A flat plain"],"a":"A deep valley","hint":"A gorge is a narrow, deep valley carved by a river.","exp":"The <b>Black River Gorges</b> is a deep valley carved by rivers through the upland rock. It is the largest national park in Mauritius and is important for biodiversity."},{"id":"g7sms-islands-008","q":"Why do volcanic islands like Mauritius generally have higher elevations than coral islands?","opts":["Lava piles up into peaks","Winds are stronger there","They are older than reefs","Coral is heavier than rock"],"a":"Lava piles up into peaks","hint":"Lava can build up significantly over time.","exp":"<b>Volcanic islands</b> have significant relief (mountains, ridges, peaks) because they are built from layers of solidified lava that pile up over millions of years. Mauritius has peaks like Le Pouce (811m) and Pieter Both (820m)."},{"id":"g7sms-islands-012","q":"Which characteristic distinguishes a continental island from a volcanic or coral island?","opts":["It was built up by coral","It was built up by lava","It has no beaches at all","It broke off a continent"],"a":"It broke off a continent","hint":"Madagascar is an example of a continental island — it was once part of Africa.","exp":"A <b>continental island</b> is a piece of a continent that became separated from the mainland as sea levels rose or through continental drift. It differs from volcanic islands (formed by lava) and coral islands (formed by reef growth)."}],"g7sms-origins":[{"id":"g7sms-origins-017","q":"The Creole community of Mauritius is mainly descended from:","opts":["Indian indentured workers","Chinese traders and shopkeepers","French plantation owners","Enslaved Africans and Malagasy"],"a":"Enslaved Africans and Malagasy","hint":"The word \"Creole\" in this context refers to those of African/Malagasy heritage born in Mauritius.","exp":"The <b>Creole community</b> of Mauritius is mainly descended from <b>enslaved Africans and Malagasy people</b> brought to Mauritius during the French and British colonial periods. Kreol Morisien, the national language, developed from their linguistic contact with the French colonists."},{"id":"g7sms-origins-006","q":"Indian indentured labourers began arriving in Mauritius from:","opts":["1834","1968","1638","1715"],"a":"1834","hint":"They arrived as slavery was being brought to an end.","exp":"The first Indian indentured labourers landed at <b>Aapravasi Ghat in 1834</b>, months before slavery was abolished on 1 February 1835 — the planters were already arranging the labour that would replace it. They came under contract to work the sugar plantations, and today people of Indian origin form the majority of the Mauritian population."},{"id":"g7sms-origins-014","q":"The Treaty of Paris in 1814 was significant for Mauritius because:","opts":["It gave Mauritius to Britain","It ended all sugar taxes","It opened the first schools","It granted independence"],"a":"It gave Mauritius to Britain","hint":"Treaties after wars formalise the transfer of territories.","exp":"The <b>Treaty of Paris (1814)</b> formally handed Mauritius — captured by Britain in 1810 during the Napoleonic Wars — from France to Britain. The British were allowed to keep it in exchange for restoring other French territories. This began the British period (1810–1968)."},{"id":"g7sms-origins-015","q":"During the French period, Mauritius (Île de France) was important to France primarily as:","opts":["A holiday spot for the rich","A centre of French schooling","A source of gold and diamonds","A naval base for French fleets"],"a":"A naval base for French fleets","hint":"The French navy used Mauritius to challenge British control of Indian Ocean trade.","exp":"The French valued Mauritius (Île de France) as a key <b>naval and strategic base</b>. French privateers and warships used Port-Louis as their base to disrupt British trade in the Indian Ocean. This strategic value made the island worth capturing during the Napoleonic Wars."},{"id":"g7sms-origins-011","q":"The concept of \"Mauritian identity\" suggests that:","opts":["Only the island-born belong","Everyone shares one origin","Shared nation, many roots","Ethnic group alone decides"],"a":"Shared nation, many roots","hint":"The national motto of Mauritius is \"Stella Clavisque Maris Indici\" (Star and Key of the Indian Ocean).","exp":"<b>Mauritian identity</b> is the shared sense of belonging to the Mauritian nation — a common identity that transcends ethnic, religious and cultural differences. It is built on shared history, citizenship and a commitment to living together."}],"g7sms-people":[{"id":"g7sms-people-015","q":"The ravane is an instrument central to which Mauritian cultural tradition?","opts":["Chinese opera","Classical Western music","Indian classical music","Sega music and dance"],"a":"Sega music and dance","hint":"The ravane is a traditional Mauritian drum.","exp":"The <b>ravane</b> is a large, shallow frame drum central to <b>Sega music</b>, Mauritius's traditional music form with African roots. The ravane's rhythm, combined with the maravane (rattle) and triangle, creates the distinctive sound of Sega."},{"id":"g7sms-people-014","q":"Bruno Julie contributed to Mauritius as:","opts":["A boxer","A painter","A politician","A biologist"],"a":"A boxer","hint":"He brought home the first Olympic medal Mauritius had ever won.","exp":"<b>Bruno Julie</b> won a <b>bronze medal in boxing</b> at the <b>2008 Beijing Olympics</b> — the first Olympic medal ever won by Mauritius. He competed in the bantamweight division."},{"id":"g7sms-people-002","q":"Mauritius has produced world-class athletes. In which sport did Stephan Buckland achieve international success?","opts":["Cricket","Football","Athletics","Swimming"],"a":"Athletics","hint":"He competed at the Olympics and Commonwealth Games as a sprinter.","exp":"<b>Stephan Buckland</b> is a celebrated Mauritian sprinter who competed at the <b>Commonwealth Games and Olympic Games</b>. He is one of the best-known Mauritian athletes internationally."},{"id":"g7sms-people-017","q":"Why is it important that Mauritius celebrates contributions from people of all communities?","opts":["It honours the majority only","It matters only for exams","It is required by world law","It builds a shared identity"],"a":"It builds a shared identity","hint":"Inclusive recognition of all communities strengthens national unity.","exp":"Celebrating contributions from <b>all communities</b> (Creole, Indian, Chinese, European) reinforces a <b>shared Mauritian identity</b>, reduces communal tensions and acknowledges that nation-building is a collective achievement — not the work of one group alone."},{"id":"g7sms-people-009","q":"Why is it important to learn about the contributions of Mauritians from all communities?","opts":["It builds pride in one nation","It helps us win at sport","The law requires us to do so","Only one group contributed"],"a":"It builds pride in one nation","hint":"Learning about diverse contributors promotes a sense of shared heritage.","exp":"Learning about contributions from <b>all communities</b> — Creole, Indian, Chinese, European — builds national pride, promotes <b>social cohesion</b> and shows that every cultural group has played a role in building Mauritius."}]}}};
